    "briefing": "The batch makes preparation CPU belong to the search that starts shared preparation, rather than every search that reuses it. In packages/harness/src/graph-search.ts, the cached preparation result gains resource attributes without shared caller ownership; preparation and search handling collect command usage, annotate the preparation span, return ownership per invocation, and attach preparation attributes only to the initiating search’s base-ready event. CPU selection is meant to use complete answered service-child usage and otherwise retain local command totals, while preserving separate client CPU and missing-CPU reporting under the preparation accounting requirement. This relies on graph-client’s normalized observations and command callback, not provider receipt parsing in the harness. The preparation-service test supplies a temporary Git repository and fake engine to exercise initial preparation and subsequent reuse.The new integration test checks fixed service CPU and metadata, separate client CPU, and a positive command count on the first preparation event, then no preparation attributes on a second distinct search. The implementation changes describe caller-specific ownership and span annotation, but the supplied evidence includes no test execution results. The listed requirements have no linked proof establishing fallback, concurrent reuse, prewarming, retry, deadline behavior, or run-log serialization.",
