      "explained": {
        "before": "Each syntax plan or preparation copied the engine into a new temporary directory and removed it afterward. Provider, source and generation qualification surrounded the command, but results did not forward service observations. Freezing always copied the configured engine into a caller-provided temporary directory before reading its provider facts. Each snapshot preparation copied the engine into a temporary directory, indexed the requested HEAD, qualified the receipt and removed the executable copy afterward. Snapshot results exposed cache and count information without service observations.",
        "after": "Both commands use the cached copy for that build and leave it available afterward. Qualified results include normalized service data only when reported, while identity and observation checks remain. The listed test exercises sequential path reuse and receipt normalization; no run is recorded. Callers choose either a persistent cached build copy or the former temporary copy. Version and capability observation still run against that selected copy, and the returned digest function supports checking its bytes afterward. Listed tests cover sequential paths and cached-copy recovery; no run is recor…",
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "stableExecutableCopy",
            "meaning": "Finds or publishes a verified executable under the canonical cache path for its build."
          },
          {
            "term": "service",
            "meaning": "Optional normalized observations about whether the shared service answered and any resources it reported."
          },
          {
            "term": "place",
            "meaning": "The caller's explicit choice between temporary storage and stable cache storage."
          }
        ]
      },
